==============================================================================
OPEN REVIEW ITEMS
==============================================================================

  1. THIS DOCKERFILE, NOT common/common1.18.4/Dockerfile, IS WHAT IS BUILT
     The Taskfile.yml task build-common has its `dir:` line commented out
     ("# use the toplevel for common") and runs

         docker buildx build -t "{{.COMMON}}" .

     from the repository root, so the image published as
     {{.BASEIMG}}/openserverless-runtime-common:{{.COMMON_VER}}-{{.TAG}}
     is built from THIS Dockerfile. The Dockerfiles under common/common1.17.1
     and common/common1.18.4 are not reached by any task; common1.18.4's
     would not even build standalone, since it runs `go build` without first
     COPYing go.mod, the proxy sources or openwhisk/. Decide which of the
     three is authoritative and delete or wire up the others; until then the
     top-level LICENSE and NOTICE carry three blocks for what is in practice
     one image.

  2. THE PUBLISHED TAG DOES NOT MATCH ANY PER-IMAGE BANNER
     COMMON_VER is 1.18.4 and TAG is the most recent annotated git tag, so
     the pushed name is openserverless-runtime-common:1.18.4-<git tag>.
     No per-image block is banner'd with that string: this block names the
     Dockerfile and the other two name their directories (common1.17.1,
     common1.18.4). A reader of the concatenation cannot tell which block
     describes the image they pulled. Settle on the published tag as the
     banner before the release vote.

  3. THE IMAGE IS PUSHED, SO ITS FILESYSTEM IS PUBLISHED
     common/common1.17.1/WARN and common/common1.18.4/WARN both state that
     the common image "is a BUILDER stage only: the language runtime images
     copy /go/bin/proxy out of it and never ship its filesystem". That is
     true of how the runtime images CONSUME it, but build-common runs
     buildx with --push when PUSH is set, so the whole image -- Go
     toolchain, Debian bookworm userland and all -- is published to
     docker.io/apache as a convenience binary. The GPL userland disclosed
     above is therefore inside a published ASF artifact, not merely a
     transient build stage. Reconcile that sentence with what is pushed.

  4. THE COPIED LICENSE AND NOTICE ARE THE WHOLE-REPOSITORY CONCATENATION
     The line

         COPY DISCLAIMER LICENSE NOTICE /

     copies the concatenated root files, which inventory all nineteen other
     images as well as this one. The image consequently ships the licensing
     of components it does not contain. Over-disclosure is not a policy
     violation, but confirm it is intended rather than shipping only this
     image's own block.

  5. WARN IS NOT COPIED INTO THE IMAGE
     Every other Dockerfile in this repository copies four files:

         COPY DISCLAIMER LICENSE NOTICE WARN /

     This one copies only three. The NOTICE it does copy states that
     "Components flagged '*** SEE WARN ***' are listed in the WARN file as
     incompatible with the Apache License 2.0", so the published image
     points at a file that is not in it. Either add WARN to the COPY line or
     accept that the reference dangles. Note that WARN is explicitly NOT
     part of the licensing grant, so shipping it is a disclosure choice and
     not a requirement.

  6. go.sum CARRIES MODULES THAT LICENSE AND NOTICE DO NOT LIST
     `RUN go mod download` resolves the module graph, and go.sum contains
     entries absent from the require blocks of go.mod:

         github.com/coreos/go-systemd/v22 v22.5.0   (go.mod hash only)
         github.com/godbus/dbus/v5 v5.0.4           (go.mod hash only)
         github.com/pkg/errors v0.9.1               (go.mod hash only)
         github.com/rs/xid v1.5.0                   (go.mod hash only)
         gopkg.in/check.v1 v0.0.0-20161208181325    (module hash present)

     They are graph or test-only entries and are not linked into the proxy
     binary, which is why they are not in this image's LICENSE and NOTICE.
     Confirm that before the release audit with

         go version -m /go/bin/proxy
         go-licenses report ./...

     and add anything that turns out to be linked.

  7. THE BASE IMAGE FLOATS AND ITS COMMENT IS STALE
     The comment above the FROM line reads "Do not fix the patch level for
     golang:1.19 to automatically get security fixes" while the line itself
     is `FROM golang:1.21`. The intent -- floating patch level -- is sound,
     but it means the Debian userland inside the published image changes
     between builds without any change to LICENSE, NOTICE or this file. The
     inventory is a snapshot of the declared dependencies, not of a built
     image. Fix the version in the comment, and re-run the audit commands
     in warning 3 of the top-level WARN against each release build.

  8. go.mod DECLARES go 1.21.4 AGAINST A FLOATING golang:1.21
     Nothing licensing-related turns on this, but a base image bump to a Go
     release with different vendored crypto or x/sys requirements would
     change the component list. Re-check the inventory whenever the FROM
     line moves to a new minor version.
